<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somme (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as River Somme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Somme is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, flowing about 245 km west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbeville">Abbeville</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>. Known in antiquity as the Samara, it gave its name to the Gallic town of Samarobriva, later Amiens. Its valley was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-somme">Battle of the Somme</a> in 1916 and of heavy fighting in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somme-river.jpg" alt="Somme (river)" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:User:Donarreiskoffer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Somme.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
